<Base title="Lab" chapter="03 / Lab" description="How the slime mold background works.">
  <section class="panel">
    <h2>The background is alive<br />(sort of)</h2>
    <p>
      The pattern behind every page simulates <em>Physarum polycephalum</em>, a slime mold that forms efficient transport
      networks. It follows the agent model described by Jeff Jones (2010) and the GPU approach popularised
      by <a href="https://cargocollective.com/sagejenson/physarum">Sage Jenson</a>.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Each agent has a position and a heading, stored in a floating-point texture.</li>
      <li>Every step, an agent samples the trail map at three sensors (left, forward and right) and turns toward the strongest one.</li>
      <li>It moves forward and deposits a little pheromone where it lands.</li>
      <li>The trail map is blurred and decays slightly, so unused paths fade away.</li>
    </ol>
    <p style="margin:0">
      Your pointer acts as a food source, and the agents swarm toward it. Hold the pointer down and it repels them instead.
      Use <span class="mono">VIEW</span> in the bottom corner to hide the page and watch the simulation.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
